the Prolog collection was designed for  scanty scanty produces a furniture using  solid teak wood we designed this  collection with the Japanese market in  mind this was the crucial part of the  process where we made a decision to do  the research and to let the compositions  and stories with the Japanese customer  inform the design when we did the  researcher in Japan  certain insights we got such as when the  customer see more of the floor or more  of the walls true the furniture they  feel that the the space in their small  homes feel more roomy and most patients  for example the swivel bench in the  collection which a lot of times in a  small home setting a piece of furniture  is utilized for different purposes and  functions so that was what inspired us  to design a bench of the backrest that  enables you to swivel and face both  directions this kind of insights that we  get from the customers through the  research trip that we made was very  valuable in helping us shape the  eventual design the reason why I think  the collection is successful is probably  because people can relate to some of the  design decisions that we made such as  making the bookshelves  shallow instead of D making the dining  table not as deep as the usual dining  table so that you feel more spacious we  don't have a set design style or fixed  design philosophy in our studio but I  think what we tell the world is that we  don't apply any fixed formulas to our  projects which means we approach each  project on a fresh page we feel very  honored of course to win the president's  design award we would like to thank  everybody who has contributed to the  project well I think that having  accolade  such as the president's design work  definitely push people to innovate and  to have bigger goals for their designs  my advice to emerging designers is  design is not just about getting to the  end product but also the process a good  process and that will get you to the  good end result
 

